<article class="env-proposition" id="02C6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02C6">Proposition <span data-tag="02C6">10.9.10</span>.</a> Let $\overline{S}$ be the image of $S$ in $S'^{-1}A$, then $(SS')^{-1}A$ is isomorphic to $\overline{S}^{-1}(S'^{-1}A)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The map sending $x\in A$ to $x/1\in (SS')^{-1}A$ induces a map sending $x/s\in S'^{-1}A$ to $x/s \in (SS')^{-1}A$, by universal property. The image of the elements in $\overline{S}$ are invertible in $(SS')^{-1}A$. By the universal property we get a map $f : \overline{S}^{-1}(S'^{-1}A) \to (SS')^{-1}A$ which maps $(x/s')/(s/1)$ to $x/ss'$. 
    <p>
      On the other hand, the map from $A$ to $\overline{S}^{-1}(S'^{-1}A)$ sending $x\in A$ to $(x/1)/(1/1)$ also induces a map $g : (SS')^{-1}A \to \overline{S}^{-1}(S'^{-1}A)$ which sends $x/ss'$ to $(x/s')/(s/1)$, by the universal property again. It is immediately checked that $f$ and $g$ are inverse to each other, hence they are both isomorphisms. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>